Z||000000||RHS3||01-12-2001||02:35 PM||||I have been looking into buying a Fiero for a year and a half now and I am finally going to buy one. I would like to know if there is anything urgent I should know before I buy one? Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||01-12-2001||03:00 PM||||I don't know what you mean by urgent.  Keep in mind that the newest Fiero is 13 years old and that as with any 13 year old car, depending on the source of the car, it may need work.<P>I know some sources of Fieros, but none in the immediate Rochester area.  Remember you get what you pay for and in most instances a $500 Fiero is as good as a $500 Mustang.||||reg||3
Z||000002||RHS3||01-12-2001||04:20 PM||||I understand that with age comes problems and most $500 cars need work. The one I am looking at is a 1984 2m4. The owner has installed many new parts (alternater,brakes, all sensors, clutch, etc.)I am not worried about doing work to it, I would actually rather buy something that needs a little work.I was refering to anything I should look into before buying. Please E-Mail your list to me.<BR>Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by RHS3 (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero Fanatic||01-12-2001||04:31 PM||||How many miles does it have? My 84 Fiero has 216,000 and I now have to replace the timing gear. You should drop the oil pan and look for metal or plastic shavings in the oil. Check the ECM anyway and check for any stored codes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RHS3||01-12-2001||04:46 PM||||It has 120,000 miles runs excellent. All maintenance completed. The interior is a little dirty(seats, headliner) Otherwise I plan on installing 2.8 multi port out of my 1986 Firebird, sometime near future. No codes come up. It has ALL new sensors.<p>[This message has been edited by RHS3 (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
